course: dictionary
lesson: 671
title: One word, two places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A balcony is a small floor with a railing on the outside of a building, so you can stand or sit above the ground. «Never throw or drop anything from a balcony; even a small object falling from a height can seriously hurt someone below.» The rule tells you a balcony is high up, because things fall from it.
explain: The same word can mean something different in another place. In a theatre or cinema, the balcony is the upper level of seats. Context tells you which meaning is right: if people are sitting in rows high above the stage, it means the seats; if there is a railing outside a flat, it means the floor.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Never stand on a chair or climb anything on a balcony, because you could fall over the railing.» A balcony has a railing, so falling over it would be the danger. The sentence still makes sense with the meaning of a small floor with a railing.
fact: A balcony is a small floor with a railing on the outside of a building.
fact: In a theatre or cinema, the balcony is the upper level of seats.
fact: «Never throw or drop anything from a balcony» is a safety rule, because things falling from high up can badly hurt people below.
fact: Flats and hotels often have balconies, and a balcony door opens onto one.
fact: Balcony is a noun. The plural is balconies.
exercise: «A boy dropped a small bottle from a fifth-floor balcony.» Where was the bottle dropped from? | A rock by the sea | A kitchen cupboard | *A small floor with a railing outside a building | A seat in the top row of a cinema
exercise: «We sat in the balcony at the theatre, high above the stage.» What does balcony mean here? | A small floor with a railing outside a flat | A box for storing tools | A flight of stairs | *The upper level of seats in a theatre
exercise: Ella says, «Never climb on a balcony railing.» Why is this a rule? | *Because you could fall over it and get badly hurt | Because climbing makes you taller | Because the railing is only for decoration | Because railings are made of gold and must be protected
test: «Many injuries at home come from falls, so windows, balconies and furniture need care.» Which word in this sentence is the plural of balcony? | injuries | *balconies | furniture | windows
test: «The usher showed us to our seats in the balcony.» Where were the seats? | In the kitchen | *High up in the upper rows of a theatre | Outside a flat, on the floor | Under the stage floor
exam: «Never throw or drop anything from a balcony; even a small object can seriously hurt someone below.» What does this rule protect people from? | Being bored by slow shows | *Being hurt by things falling from high up | Getting wet in the rain | Losing their seats in the cinema
